WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.040
Part of what makes the Marvel Cinematic Universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.360
fun is how plotlines pay off over&nbsp;
multiple movies. But that doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.120
mean every storyline gets a proper ending.&nbsp;
Here are some that completely disappeared.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.600
At the end of "The Incredible Hulk,"&nbsp;
Tim Blake Nelson's Samuel Sterns is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.560
coerced into giving Emil Blonsky a medical&nbsp;
procedure that turns him into The Abomination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.520
As Blonsky charges out of Sterns' office,&nbsp;
he sends the scientist flying into a wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:33.200
and a small drop of Hulk's blood falls from a&nbsp;
nearby table onto a wound on Sterns' forehead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.360
The man's head begins to bubble and, just like&nbsp;
that, the origin story of Marvel Comics villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.000
The Leader is put into motion. The producers&nbsp;
of this project were hoping Sterns could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.280
return in an "Incredible Hulk" sequel with&nbsp;
The Leader's green skin and enormous cranium.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.760
However, "The Incredible Hulk" didn't light&nbsp;
the box office on fire, and there were also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.400
rights issues regarding further solo "Hulk"&nbsp;
movies, leaving a reappearance by The Leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.760
in limbo. More than a decade after "The Incredible&nbsp;
Hulk," other characters from that Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.960
Universe entry, such as General "Thunderbolt" Ross&nbsp;
and even The Abomination, have come back to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.840
a part in newer movies. But the origin story&nbsp;
of The Leader has never been followed up on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.840
and it doesn't appear to be something&nbsp;
that'll get expanded on anytime soon.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.680
In "Thor: The Dark World," a comic relief&nbsp;
character got her own comic relief sidekick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:29.200
Darcy Lewis, Jane Foster's humorous intern from&nbsp;
the first "Thor" film, got an assistant to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.640
her lug things around in the form of Ian Boothby.&nbsp;
The two have a mildly conflict-ridden dynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.440
throughout the adventure, before abruptly kissing&nbsp;
during the invasion of London by the Dark Elves.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.720
Ian's role in the MCU was extremely short-lived.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.160
He, like Jane and all of Thor's Earthbound&nbsp;
cohorts, did not return for "Thor: Ragnarok."

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.440
"Sorry to hear that Jane dumped you."

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.040
"She didn't dump me, you know. I&nbsp;
dumped her. It was a mutual dumping!"

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.280
In the years since, however, Jane Foster&nbsp;
has managed to land a comeback in "Thor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.000
Love and Thunder" while Darcy returned in&nbsp;
"WandaVision" as a full-fledged doctor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.280
Yet Darcy made no mention of Ian. Their&nbsp;
romance, and the character of Ian altogether,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.640
was apparently just not meant to&nbsp;
last very long in this franchise.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.040
In the middle of "Captain America: Civil War,"&nbsp;
Tony Stark drops a bombshell on Steve Rogers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.760
he and Pepper Potts are taking a break. After&nbsp;
the character was absent from "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.320
Age of Ultron" due to her duties to Stark&nbsp;
Industries, this confirmed that Stark was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.920
officially single again, thanks in part to his&nbsp;
non-stop adventures with the Avengers. It was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.200
significant development that also helped explain&nbsp;
why Potts was suddenly M.I.A. from the franchise.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.720
"Is Pepper here?"

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:41.920
"We're taking a break."

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.960
But this development was abruptly upended&nbsp;
in "Spider-Man: Homecoming," when Pepper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.640
Potts not only reappeared but was&nbsp;
being intimate with Stark as if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.760
nothing had ever happened. There was even a gag&nbsp;
about Stark popping the question to Potts in lieu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.120
of introducing Spider-Man in his Iron Spider&nbsp;
outfit to members of the press. The sudden loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.320
of Potts in Stark's life never got referenced&nbsp;
in the following two "Avengers" movies either,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.400
making this a strange unexplained detour in&nbsp;
the love life of two principal MCU figures.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.080
In the wake of "The Avengers," "Thor: The&nbsp;
Dark World" finds scientist Erik Selvig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:23.360
struggling to maintain a proper psychological&nbsp;
state. Having had Loki control his mind for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.920
an extended period of time, Selvig is&nbsp;
now fit to bouts of strange behavior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.640
like wandering around Stonehenge naked.&nbsp;
He's even been locked up in a psych ward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.080
which Darcy and Ian must break him out of to&nbsp;
assist Thor in his quest to defeat the Dark Elves.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.880
The lingering aftereffects of Selvig getting&nbsp;
his brain scrambled in "The Avengers" wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.280
last forever, however. When Thor went to&nbsp;
recruit his help in "Age of Ultron," Selvig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.360
was seen walking out of a college that&nbsp;
he teaches at. Not only had he returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.000
to his regular day job, but he seemed to have&nbsp;
fully recovered from his encounter with Loki.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:02.160
At the end of "Iron Man 2," weapons manufacturer&nbsp;
Justin Hammer is arrested by police officers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.760
As this happens, he calls back to&nbsp;
Pepper Potts that it isn't over.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.960
"I'm gonna be seeing you again, real soon."

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.800
Perhaps that was originally the plan. MCU films&nbsp;
don't just keep any villain alive and well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.480
after all. Keeping Hammer alive&nbsp;
and thirsting for vengeance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.440
had to be thought of as a way&nbsp;
to set up future installments.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:28.160
However, for whatever reason, Hammer has not&nbsp;
re-emerged in the MCU. "Iron Man 3" had no place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.040
for Hammer, replacing him with Killian, while&nbsp;
other movies starring Tony Stark, like "Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:39.280
America: Civil War," also found no room for him.&nbsp;
The most peculiar part of this is that Rockwell's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.560
profile has increased substantially since "Iron&nbsp;
Man 2" came out. Having won a Best Supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.920
Actor Academy Award and getting another Oscar&nbsp;
nomination in the same category, one would think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:55.200
Marvel Studios would be scrambling to bring him&nbsp;
back. Instead, Rockwell's entire presence in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:01.040
franchise has been limited to "Iron Man 2" and a&nbsp;
tiny cameo in the short film "All Hail the King."

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:06.320
In "The Incredible Hulk," viewers&nbsp;
aren't just introduced to the Hulk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.200
They also get a first glimpse of the franchise's&nbsp;
version of the romance between Bruce Banner and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:16.480
Betty Ross. This is a relationship complicated by&nbsp;
Banner watching out for his heart rate quickening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:22.160
out of fear of turning into the Hulk. This dynamic&nbsp;
was established as a crucial part of Banner's life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:27.760
and was even used as a signifier that there may&nbsp;
be more of Banner in the Hulk than there appeared.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.280
However, Betty has never resurfaced in the MCU&nbsp;
despite her father, General "Thunderbolt" Ross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.440
managing to re-emerge as a recurring&nbsp;
presence in recent entries in the saga.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.400
The character hasn't even been referenced&nbsp;
again, either by her father or by Banner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.560
Instead, Banner took up a romance with&nbsp;
Black Widow in "Avengers: Age of Ultron,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:51.200
which seemed to close the book on this&nbsp;
initial love affair for the Hulk's alter-ego.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.080
The only post-"Incredible Hulk" nod to the&nbsp;
character in the MCU was through the initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.760
codename for the Hulkbuster armor AI in "Age&nbsp;
of Ultron" being Veronica, a nod towards the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:06.560
Archie Comics character of the same name&nbsp;
who had a rivalry with a lady named Betty.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.720
"Captain America: The Winter&nbsp;
Soldier" ends with a genuinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.080
interesting beat. After faking&nbsp;
his death earlier in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:18.560
one would expect Fury to just return to his&nbsp;
regular day-to-day duties by the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:24.240
feature. Instead, Fury makes the conscious choice&nbsp;
to remain deceased to the general public. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:30.480
viewer even gets a glimpse at his headstone in a&nbsp;
Washington D.C. cemetery. As Fury walks off-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.840
in "The Winter Soldier," the possibilities&nbsp;
for what he could be up to next are limitless.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:41.200
Strangely, then, "Age of Ultron" brings things&nbsp;
right back to their original status quo. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.200
initially Fury is depicted as someone&nbsp;
who's been off the grid for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.840
he's eventually shown to be manning a Helicarrier&nbsp;
in his traditional leather garb to help Sokovian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.880
civilians get to safety. Rather than a shadowy&nbsp;
individual believed to be dead by most,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.560
Fury is once again a public&nbsp;
figure that everyone knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.760
is alive and well. It's a strange move that&nbsp;
undercuts the ending of "The Winter Soldier"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.160
and all the narrative possibilities that&nbsp;
Fury remaining dead could have brought.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.440
In the initial "Guardians of the Galaxy" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:16.880
Xandar is an important planet. It's where&nbsp;
four of the five titular superheroes meet up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.520
as well as home to the headquarters of the Nova&nbsp;
Corps organization, and the backdrop for Ronan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.080
the Accuser's massive final battle to execute&nbsp;
his vengeance against the planet's occupants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:31.280
Given all that and the fact that it housed an&nbsp;
Infinity Stone at the end of "Guardians of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:37.200
Galaxy," fans might expect such a location&nbsp;
and its characters, including Nova Prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:43.040
and Rhomann Dey, to reappear throughout future&nbsp;
MCU adventures, particularly cosmic-based ones.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.640
Instead, the planet hasn't reappeared at&nbsp;
all in subsequent films. The Nova Corps gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:53.680
name-dropped in "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol.&nbsp;
2", while Xandar also gets mentioned in "Thor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:58.400
Ragnarok" and "Avengers: Infinity War."&nbsp;
That's the extent that this planet has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:02.800
factored into the franchise post-"Guardians&nbsp;
of the Galaxy," with even its destruction by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:08.400
Thanos happening entirely off-screen. Xandar&nbsp;
started as a seemingly important place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:12.960
but it's been largely brushed aside in the&nbsp;
mythology of the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:17.360
Starting with the show's second&nbsp;
season, Bobbi Morse was a critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:21.680
part of "Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D." and&nbsp;
its ever-expanding mythology. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.480
she and her love interest Lance Hunter eventually&nbsp;
left "S.H.I.E.L.D." in the third season episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.600
"Parting Shot." In-universe, they were forced to&nbsp;
leave to keep Coulson and company safe. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:36.480
beyond these fictitious confines, Morse's&nbsp;
sudden absence was done to allow herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:40.560
and Hunter to headline a spin-off&nbsp;
show entitled "Marvel's Most Wanted."

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:44.400
"They're not coming back. They've been disavowed."

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.280
"What're you talking about?"

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:46.720
"There were no good options."

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:52.320
However, those plans hit a snag when ABC opted to&nbsp;
pass on "Most Wanted," leaving the two characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.800
in a state of limbo. Though Hunter managed to&nbsp;
return in a guest appearance capacity in a later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:00.720
episode of "S.H.I.E.L.D.," Morse remained&nbsp;
M.I.A. for the rest of the program's run.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:04.720
Part of this appears to be due to scheduling&nbsp;
conflicts for actress Adrienne Palicki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:08.880
who eventually found a lead role on the&nbsp;
Fox sci-fi series "The Orville." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:12.240
no official reason has ever been&nbsp;
given for her sudden departure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.160
Whatever caused the character's absence, a&nbsp;
hole was left in the "Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:20.240
mythology that kept all storylines&nbsp;
related to Morse awkwardly unresolved.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:26.640
For "Ant-Man and the Wasp," the two titular tiny&nbsp;
heroes faced off not against Thanos, or He Who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:32.560
Remains, or Ultron, but rather Ghost, a woman who&nbsp;
can move through objects and constantly looks like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:37.520
she's glitching in and out of reality. Ghost&nbsp;
turned out to be a morally complicated figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:42.720
one who merely wanted to cure her condition, not&nbsp;
destroy the world. While she wasn't above doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:47.200
bad things, she was also somebody whose&nbsp;
life wasn't defined by acts of malice.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:51.520
A scene at the end of "Ant-Man and the Wasp"&nbsp;
suggested that she and her surrogate father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:56.480
figure Bill Foster would be sticking together for&nbsp;
whatever came next. But Ghost has ended up being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.960
absent so far from the rest of the Marvel&nbsp;
Cinematic Universe. Despite Ant-Man even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:05.680
name-dropping her during the mid-credits scene of&nbsp;
"Ant-Man and the Wasp," Ghost has never appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:09.920
in another MCU entry. It also doesn't look like&nbsp;
Ghost will get her storyline resolved in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:14.640
upcoming "Ant-Man and the Wasp: Quantumania"&nbsp;
either — there's been no news on Ghost star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:19.360
Hannah John-Kamen getting cast in that film,&nbsp;
plus she's committed to starring in "Red Sonja."

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:24.880
In the middle of "Spider-Man: Homecoming," the&nbsp;
young webcrawler's inexperience with properly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:28.880
handling criminals is made apparent through&nbsp;
the bungling of his attempt to stop a weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:33.440
sale on a ferry. This transaction involves&nbsp;
the henchman of the film's primary villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:38.960
Adrian Toomes, aka The Vulture, but it also&nbsp;
entails Mac Gargan a criminal who, in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:44.480
is also known as the supervillain The Scorpion.&nbsp;
Here, Gargan is depicted as just a regular person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:48.720
albeit one with a scorpion tattoo on his&nbsp;
neck. He quickly vanishes in the chaos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.280
of Spider-Man's botched attempt to stop the crime.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:54.880
The mid-credits scene of "Homecoming,"&nbsp;
in which a battered and vengeful Gargan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:59.520
confronts Toomes in prison and asks him if&nbsp;
he knows the secret identity of Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:03.680
seemed to be setting up the idea of Scorpion&nbsp;
as a recurring threat to Spider-Man. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:07.600
Gargan has made no further appearances&nbsp;
in either "Spider-Man: Far From Home"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:12.080
or other Sony/Marvel productions like "Venom."&nbsp;
There's also been no word on him appearing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:15.840
future productions like "Spider-Man: No&nbsp;
Way Home," while a rumored presence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.480
the feature "Silver and Black" went nowhere&nbsp;
once that film got canned in pre-production.

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:24.480
While Gargan was useful in showcasing&nbsp;
Spidey's inexperience in "Homecoming,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:27.920
the seeming lack of interest in&nbsp;
further exploring the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:31.760
indicates his usefulness in the&nbsp;
MCU may have come to a close.

